    "id": "therm-mcq-397",
    "questionNumber": 397,
    "question": "The efficiency of a Carnot engine operating with source at $127^\\circ\\text{C}$ and sink at $27^\\circ\\text{C}$ is:",
    "options": {
      "A": "$25\\%$",
      "B": "$50\\%$",
      "C": "$75\\%$",
      "D": "$20\\%$"
    },
    "optionsList": [
      "$25\\%$",
      "$50\\%$",
      "$75\\%$",
      "$20\\%$"
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nTemperatures must be converted to absolute scale (Kelvin): $T(\\text{K}) = t(^\\circ\\text{C}) + 273$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nSource temperature $t_1 = 127^\\circ\\text{C} = 400\\text{ K}$, sink temperature $t_2 = 27^\\circ\\text{C} = 300\\text{ K}$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\n\\eta = 1 - \\frac{T_2}{T_1} = \\frac{T_1 - T_2}{T_1}\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Convert temperatures: $T_1 = 127 + 273 = 400\\text{ K}$, $T_2 = 27 + 273 = 300\\text{ K}$.\n2. Compute efficiency: $\\eta = 1 - \\frac{300}{400} = 1 - 0.75 = 0.25 = 25\\%$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: 25%\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE INCORRECT:\nOption D calculates (127-27)/127 in Celsius directly, which is invalid.",
